From 3f500d5741ffaf4a2725438d0970ba3c5168831b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ericek111 <erik.brocko@letemsvetemapplem.eu>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:14:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 27/47] plugin: Apple's GL renderer draws without a compute
 pass per draw (restart and provoking-vertex SPI)

AppleMetalOpenGLRenderer (GL 4.1 over Metal: Minecraft and every other GL app) rewrote each draw's index buffer with
a compute dispatch (fixIndexBuffer) before drawing, so every GL draw cost a draw plus a dispatch (~13 us; Minecraft
issues ~50k draws a frame and ran at 1 fps). Two reasons, from its disassembly:

- It carries GL's primitive restart state through the AGX encoder SPI setPrimitiveRestartEnabled:[index:] and logged
  it unsupported. The render encoder now takes it: strips restart at the all-ones index when GL enables restart (Vulkan
  restarts only there, GL's usual index), never otherwise; lists never restart.
- It picks a capability table by the device name, and "Intel" (0x144) lacks bit 3, "encoder takes
  setProvokingVertexMode:", so GL's default last-vertex convention was emulated by rotating every triangle's indices.
  The device now enables VK_EXT_provoking_vertex last-vertex mode as EDS3 dynamic state, the encoder takes
  setProvokingVertexMode: (non-zero = last), and when the renderer first asks for the device name the bit is set in
  its in-memory Intel table (found by its exact bytes followed by the NVIDIA table; anything else is left alone).

Fans, quads and polygons are still rewritten (Metal has no such primitives). vm/guest/tools/glsem checks flat
shading under both conventions and strips with restart on and off: all pass. glbench (2000 small draws): 25.2 ->
7.3 ms per frame, same checksum. Minecraft: 1 -> 2 fps (the rest is per-draw cost).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
